  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1025 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe pattern does not change much through Thursday. The main \ntheme during that period is the upper riding strengthens and we \nget hotter again. Highs climb back into the lower 90s again by \nThursday. With weak southerly flow continuing it will remain humid \nin the afternoon. Heat index values will climb back into the 95 to\n100 degree range in warmer locations. \n\nBy Thursday night into Friday, a strong longwave trough axis over\nthe desert southwest pushes east into the Midwestern states and\nweakens. At the same time the center of the upper ridge shift \neast off the coast of the Carolinas and Florida. Weak convergence\nbetween the upper level trough axis and the western edge of the \nupper high should provide some forcing over the area. This should\nlead to some increasing cloud cover and possibly some isolated to\nscattered shower and thunderstorm activity. \n\nHowever, models differ on how much/strength of forcing and thus\nresultant precipitation that develops over the southeast on \nFriday into early portions of the day on Saturday. Given high PWAT\nvalues if forcing is stronger, Saturday and Sunday could both be \na washout with thunderstorms possible. However, if the drier \nsolution holds, isolated to scattered coverage may be the result \non Friday and Saturday with the washout reserved for Sunday primarily.\nHeat may be a concern again on Saturday if the drier solution \npans out. Highs will remain in the upper 80s to lower 90s if so \nwith heat index values between 95 and 100 degrees. The rainy \nsolution would end up providing much cooler temperatures for \nFriday (~lower to mid 80s).",
